{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"dicey-acute-finwhale","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Symptom\n\nThree tests fail intermittently in `make verify` and in CI, in different combinations each\nrun. Observed in a full `make verify` at `origin/main`:\n\n```\nFAILED tests/interfaces/facades/test_plan_review_attestation_facades.py::test_cli_passed_but_unsigned_is_exit_11_with_byte_identical_stderr\nFAILED tests/interfaces/facades/test_verify_completion_facades.py::test_cli_llm_error_is_clean_exit_one\n3 failed, 20648 passed, 27 skipped in 8293.97s\n```\n\nEach passes when run alone. The failure is produced by test ORDER, not by any one test.\n\n## Reproduction (deterministic)\n\nRun the two facade tests in that order, on a clean `origin/main` tree:\n\n```\npytest tests/interfaces/facades/test_plan_review_attestation_facades.py::test_cli_passed_but_unsigned_is_exit_11_with_byte_identical_stderr \\\n       tests/interfaces/facades/test_verify_completion_facades.py::test_cli_llm_error_is_clean_exit_one \\\n       -p no:randomly -p no:cacheprovider\n```\n\nThe second fails. Reversing or isolating them passes. Reproduced at `7c3cb5e9a4f`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`src/rebar/_logging.py:81` constructs the handler as\n`logging.StreamHandler(sys.stderr)`, which binds the stream OBJECT at construction time.\n`install_stderr_handler` is deliberately idempotent (`_HANDLER_MARKER`, `:74-77`): once a\nhandler is installed on the `rebar` logger, every later call returns early and the original\nhandler — with its original stream — stays for the life of the process.\n\nThat is correct for production, where a process has one stderr for its lifetime. It is wrong\nwhenever `sys.stderr` is REPLACED mid-process. Under pytest, `capsys` swaps `sys.stderr` per\ntest and closes the buffer at teardown. So:\n\n1. The first test to call `main()` installs a handler bound to THAT test's capsys buffer.\n2. The test ends; pytest closes the buffer.\n3. A later test calls `main()`. The marker short-circuits the install, so the stale handler\n   survives.\n4. `rebar._commands._seam:357` emits its \"authorship: signing SKIPPED\" warning. The handler\n   writes to the closed buffer:\n\n```\n--- Logging error ---\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \".../logging/__init__.py\", line 1163, in emit\n    stream.write(msg + self.terminator)\nValueError: I/O operation on closed file.\n```\n\n5. `logging` prints that report to the CURRENT `sys.stderr`, which the later test is\n   capturing. Its assertion `\"Traceback\" not in err` therefore fails on a traceback that\n   belongs to the logging subsystem, not to the command under test.\n\nWhich tests collide depends on which one calls `main()` first, so under `-n 4 --dist\nworksteal` the failing set varies run to run. That is the intermittency.\n\nThis is not a test-only defect. Any embedder that redirects `sys.stderr` after the first\nrebar entrypoint call — a library consumer, a wrapping CLI, a captured subprocess harness —\nkeeps writing to the original stream.\n\n## The fix\n\nResolve `sys.stderr` at EMIT time instead of binding it at construction. The idempotency\nmarker stays (it exists to stop duplicate handlers stacking, and that is still wanted); what\nchanges is that the one installed handler stops pinning a stream that can die underneath it.\n\nThis deletes the failure class rather than reordering or isolating the tests, and adds no\nmechanism of any ratcheted kind — it is a handler that reads a global at emit time instead of\nat construction.\n\n## Not the fix\n\n- Marking the tests as order-dependent, or pinning their order. That hides an observable\n  defect in the shipped logging path.\n- Resetting handlers in a fixture. That is a new `autouse_fixture` — a ratcheted mechanism\n  kind — installed to work around a library bug, and it would leave the embedder case broken.\n- `recheck` in CI. The project rule is explicit: a flaky test is a bug to root-cause, never a\n  retry.\n\n## The third failure\n\n`tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py::test_uncoordinated_parallel_fetches_can_race_but_coordination_prevents_it`\nalso failed in the same run. It has not been diagnosed yet and may be an unrelated race.\nTriage it separately before assuming it shares this cause.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The reproduction above passes: the two facade tests pass when run in that order, on\n      that command line.\n- [ ] Each of the two still passes when run alone, and the full\n      `tests/interfaces/facades/` directory passes.\n- [ ] A regression test fails without the fix and passes with it, asserting that a warning\n      emitted after `sys.stderr` is replaced reaches the NEW stream — not that the tests\n      happen to pass in some order.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_fetch_common_dir_coordination.py::test_uncoordinated_parallel_fetches_can_race_but_coordination_prevents_it`\n      is diagnosed: either fixed here, or filed as its own bug with evidence that it is a\n      different cause.\n- [ ] No new `autouse_fixture`, `env_var`, `config_key`, `feature_flag`, `ci_gate`, `lock` or\n      `test_helper` is added; `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0.\n- [ ] `make verify` passes.\n\n## Provider neutrality\n\nNo model, vendor, CI provider or harness is involved. `_logging.py` is in-process and reads\nno CI environment, so the fix behaves identically under any CI provider or none.","id":"7773-cca2-da72-4a0c","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Stale stderr handler makes CLI facade tests order-dependent"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793825980297293665,"uuid":"f0f7838f-2423-42ee-add4-706bf19a37d8"}